ान्त को मान भी लें तो शरीर परिवर्तन होने\nका तात्कालिक कारण मन की चंचलता होगी।\n\nअंश के सम्बन्ध में टिप्पणी—इस अध्याय में वर्णन हुआ है कि कृष्ण अंशेन अर्थात् अपने अंशों",
      "textEn": "76\n\nIn reality, the sun and moon reflected in the water do not exist, yet according to the movements of the water,\ntheir reflection keeps falling. Similarly, when our minds are disturbed, we wander in various\nphysical environments and acquire different kinds of bodies. In the Bhagavad Gita, it is called Guna-\nSanga. The reason is his/her qualities. Madhwacharya's statement is—Gunanubaddhahsan. And Shri Chaitanya\nMahaprabhu says—Brahmanda bhramite kon bhagyavan jeev (Chaitanya Charitamrita 19.151). The living universe\nkeeps circling in the fill, sometimes in heaven, sometimes in the middle world and lower worlds, sometimes in human form, sometimes as a god, dog, tree, etc. This is because of the fickleness of the mind. Therefore, the mind\nshould be stabilized. As it is said—Sa vai manah krishnapadaravindayoh. Man should\nthat he fixes his mind on the lotus feet of Krishna. Then he can get rid of his restlessness. This is the teaching of the Garuda Purana, and the same thing is repeated in the Naradiya Purana.\nIn the Bhagavad Gita,\nDevavrata is said to be a devotee of God. The restless mind goes to different worlds because it remains attached to different\ngods. But by worshiping these gods, he cannot go to the abode of the Lord.\nBecause it is not confirmed by any Vedic literature. Man is his own master. In this human life, he has the privilege to understand his true state and\ndetermine whether he has to keep circling the universe continuously or return to the abode of God. This is also confirmed in the Bhagavad Gita (Aprapya ma na nivartante mrityum cha ya vartaman). There is no such thing as coincidence. If a tree is burning from the fire of a claim, and in a distant tree\na fire breaks out, but the nearby tree survives, then it seems like a coincidence. Similarly, humans\nare seen to obtain different types of bodies by chance, but in reality, they are obtained because of the body and mind.\nThe mind remains in a state of duality between acceptance and rejection, and according to this acceptance-\nrejection by the mind, we receive different types of bodies, although it seems from above\nthat it happens only by coincidence. If we accept this principle of coincidence, then the immediate cause of the body changing\nwill be the fickleness of the mind. Note regarding the fraction—This chapter describes that Krishna, in the sense of fraction, that is, his fractions"
