      "textEn": "89\n\nEncouraged the cons. Narada Muni is the constant well-wisher of human society. They wanted that\nAs soon as possible, Krishna should descend on this earth so that the society of gods is pleased, and Kansa\nand his friends should witness the killing by Krishna. Kansa also attained salvation from his misdeeds. From this,\nthe gods and their followers will be greatly pleased. In this regard, Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti Thakur's comment is that Narad Muni sometimes used to do such deeds that were beneficial to the gods and demons together. In this context, Shri Veer Raghav Acharya has included the following half verse in his commentary—\n\nAshurah sarva evait lokopadravakarinah\n\nThe Asura community has always been a troublesome element for human society. Thus, under the tenth canto of Srimad Bhagavatam, \"The introduction to the incarnation of Lord Krishna:\"\nThe Bhakti Vedanta Tatparya, the first chapter, is completed.\nChapter two.\n\nPraise of the unborn Krishna by the gods.\n\nIn this chapter, it is explained that when God entered Devaki's womb to kill Kansa, all the gods understood that God was residing in Devaki's womb, therefore the gods\npraised the womb in their honor. Kansa, under the protection of his father-in-law Jarasandha, with the help of demons like Prahlad, Bak, Chanur, Trunavarta, Aghasura, Mushtik, Bana, and\nBhoomasura, started tormenting the Yaduvanshis. Therefore, the Yadavas, leaving their respective\nhomes, went to the refuge of kingdoms like Kuru, Panchal, Kekaya, Shalva, and Vidarbha. Some of them,\nHe, who was a nominal friend, remained with Kansa. When Kansa killed Devaki's six sons (six-born) one by one, Anantadeva entered Devaki's womb,\nand by the trick of Yogmaya, who obeyed the order of God, they were transferred into Rohini's womb. The one who will soon appear as the eighth son of Devaki"
